cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or build on this work, please cite it as below."
title: "The Clean Two-Query Threshold for Unitary Synthesis"
authors:
  - family-names: "Douglas"
    given-names: "Seth"
version: 1.0.1
date-released: 2026-09-11
type: software
repository-code: "https://github.com/Apsiape/two-query-unitary-synthesis"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - quantum query complexity
  - unitary synthesis
  - Aaronson-Kuperberg problem
  - Gaussian mean width
  - matrix concentration
  - Lean 4
abstract: >-
  A tight characterization of clean two-query unitary synthesis. Lower bound: for any
  fixed two-query architecture with arbitrary contractions and independent unimodular
  phase selectors, the Gaussian mean width of the realizable family is at most
  2 min(N,Q) sqrt(log 2Q), so universal clean two-query synthesis of n-qubit unitaries
  needs Omega(2^n) qubits and, by an address-compression lemma, Omega(2^n) oracle input
  bits regardless of workspace. Upper bound: a permutation-transport construction
  implements every unitary cleanly with two Boolean queries to one table,
  O(2^n log(1/eps)) qubits and address bits. Both thresholds are Theta(2^n) up to
  log(1/eps). The bound is tight in both factors at workspace Theta(N^2); the one-query
  argument does not extend; and the garbage-model two-query problem is reduced to a clean
  four-insertion width bound, which remains open. Eight deterministic verifiers and a
  kernel-checked Lean spine accompany the paper.
